{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The filtered list that showed items you'd just deleted</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      You've got a list of items and a search box. Instead of filtering on every render, you store the filtered result in its own piece of state, updated by an effect whenever the items or the search text changes. It mostly works, until someone deletes an item and the filtered list still shows it for a moment, or forever if the effect's dependency array is slightly wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bug isn't really in the effect. It's that <code>filteredItems</code> exists as its own state at all, two things pretending to be independent when one is really just a computation over the other.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Storing a value that's fully computable from other state (<code>filteredItems</code> from <code>items</code> + <code>searchText</code>) creates a second source of truth. It's fine right up until one update path forgets to trigger the recompute, at which point the two go out of sync and nothing in the type system or the framework tells you.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is a category error, not a missing dependency. A <code>useEffect</code> that exists solely to keep one state variable in sync with another is a sign the derived value shouldn't be state in the first place, it should be an expression evaluated during render.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>If you can compute it, don't store it</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Instead of keeping <code>filteredItems</code> as its own <code>useState</code>, just calculate it directly from <code>items</code> and <code>searchText</code> every time the component renders. There's nothing to "keep in sync" because there's only ever one value, computed fresh, never stale.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      <code>const filteredItems = items.filter(i => i.name.includes(searchText));</code> run inline during render replaces both the extra state variable and the effect that used to update it. If the computation is expensive, wrap it in <code>useMemo</code> for performance, not correctness, the memoized version still recomputes whenever its dependencies change; it just skips redoing the work when they haven't.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The general rule: state should hold the smallest set of values that can't be derived from anything else already in state or props. Everything else is a view over that minimal set, computed on demand. This eliminates an entire class of bugs by construction, there's no code path where the derived value can diverge from its inputs, because it's never assigned independently of them.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The dashboard total that didn't match the rows</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team's dashboard showed a list of transactions and a running total, kept as separate state, the total updated by an effect whenever the list changed. A support ticket came in: the total on screen didn't match the sum of the visible rows. Nobody could figure out why from the code alone, it looked correct.
    </p>
    <p>
      The real cause: a bulk-delete action removed several rows in one state update, but the effect updating the total was debounced for performance and fired before the delete had fully propagated through a second piece of derived state the total actually depended on.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The fix removed the total as stored state entirely and replaced it with <code>transactions.reduce((sum, t) => sum + t.amount, 0)</code> computed inline. The debounce, the effect, and the dependency-array tuning all disappeared along with the bug, there was no longer a window where the total and the list could disagree.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The team generalized this into a lightweight review rule: any <code>useEffect</code> whose only job is <code>setSomeState(computeFromOtherState())</code> is treated as a smell during code review, not automatically wrong, but requiring a one-line justification for why the value can't just be computed during render instead.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

