{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>When derived state actually earns its keep</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Not every computed value should skip <code>useState</code>. If a value needs to persist even after its original inputs change or disappear (like "the last search result before the list was cleared"), that's a real, independent piece of state, not something to compute inline.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The test is whether the value has meaning independent of its current inputs. If it's always "whatever you'd get from recomputing right now," it's derived. If it needs to remember something the current inputs no longer contain, it's genuinely separate state.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This same anti-pattern shows up outside component state too: a Redux selector that's memoized correctly is the derived-state pattern done right; a reducer that duplicates data another slice already owns, updated by a second action creator "to keep it in sync," is the exact same bug at the store level, and just as fixable by deleting the duplicate and selecting instead.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

